@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: Political Thought and Trajectory -- Russian Social Democracy, Menshevism, and WWI
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-07-16
PAPERS_FOUND: 10 total (High: 4, Medium: 5, Low: 1)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain traces Plekhanov's political biography and its entanglement with his
theoretical commitments, from the 1883 founding of the Emancipation of Labour
group through the 1903 RSDLP split, the 1905 and 1917 revolutions, and his
"defencist" stance in World War I. Encyclopedia searches (SEP, IEP) turned up no
dedicated treatment of Plekhanov's politics or of Menshevism as such -- SEP's
coverage of Russian Marxism runs through Lenin, Luxemburg, Gramsci, and Lukacs,
and IEP has no entries on the Mensheviks, the RSDLP split, or the Emancipation of
Labour group. The literature here is therefore drawn almost entirely from
historical and Marxological journals (International Review of Social History,
Revolutionary Russia, Historical Materialism, International Critical Thought,
Studies in East European Thought) rather than philosophy venues proper.

Two clusters of scholarship dominate. The first concerns party organization: how
the Emancipation of Labour group's early polemics against Narodnik terrorism
(Gaido and Bosch Alessio on Zasulich) fed into the Iskra group's model of a
centralized "cadre party" (Gaido and Jozami), and how pre-war unity conferences
(Elwood on the 1914 Brussels conference) tried and failed to reconcile the
resulting Bolshevik/Menshevik factions. The second concerns the "two-stage"
schema of Russian revolution: Plekhanov's 1906 questionnaire to international
Social Democracy on the class character of the 1905 Revolution (Avila and
Gaido), Kautsky's dissenting reply that undercut the Menshevik position from
within the Second International, and the retrospective debate over "permanent
revolution" as a rival schema to Menshevik stagism (Day). Baron's 1958 and 1981
articles -- distinct from his general biography already treated in the
philosophy-of-history domain -- anchor, respectively, the theoretical question
of Russia's place in a Marxist stages-of-development scheme and the empirical
history of Plekhanov's wartime patriotism. Thatcher and the Ascher review of
Tiutiukin's monograph supply Menshevik-internal and Soviet-historiographical
perspectives, respectively, on 1905.

A recurring interpretive puzzle threads through this literature without being
fully resolved by any single piece: the relationship between Plekhanov's
historical determinism (developed at length in his philosophy of history) and
his repeated political improvisation at decisive moments -- his 1883 break with
Narodism, his 1903 alignment with Martov against Lenin over party rules, his
1914 defencism against the bulk of international socialism. None of the papers
gathered here treats this determinism/voluntarism tension as its central
subject; it emerges as a background problem that synthesis must reconstruct
from the historical narrative.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the empirical political narrative -- Emancipation of
Labour, the 1903 split, the two-stage theory of 1905, and 1914 defencism --
that the project's key questions ask to be read against Plekhanov's philosophy
of history (researched separately) and against Lenin's philosophical
trajectory (researched separately, in domain 5). It is deliberately restricted
to the organizational and political dimension of the Lenin split, leaving
Lenin's own philosophical debts to Plekhanov to the influence domain.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
No SEP or IEP article addresses Menshevism, the RSDLP split, or the Emancipation
of Labour group directly, so this domain lacks encyclopedia-level framing
entirely (Stage 5.6 encyclopedia-context extraction was skipped for this
reason). Coverage of Plekhanov's WWI "defencism" rests on a single (though
substantial) Baron article; no dedicated recent scholarly treatment of his
Zimmerwald-era polemics was located. Axelrod's and Martov's own theoretical
contributions to Menshevism are visible here only at second hand (via Moodie-
and Getzler-adjacent searches that did not yield a citable, abstracted primary
analysis), so the movement's other principal theorists remain underrepresented
relative to Plekhanov himself. Two entries (Baron 1958 and the Ascher review of
Tiutiukin) could not be enriched with an abstract from any source (S2, OpenAlex,
CORE) and are marked INCOMPLETE; they remain in this file for reference but are
excluded from synthesis.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Synthesis should use this domain to narrate the sequence Emancipation of Labour
-> Iskra -> 1903 split -> 1905 two-stage schema -> 1914 defencism as the
political counterpart to the deterministic philosophy of history developed
elsewhere, and should draw explicitly on Avila/Gaido and Day to show how
Plekhanov's own 1906 canvassing of international Social Democracy exposed
Menshevik stagism to an internal challenge (Kautsky) well before Trotsky's
rival theory of permanent revolution became the dominant left alternative.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Organizational/Iskra-centralist reading: 2 papers (Gaido and Jozami; Gaido and
  Bosch Alessio) -- traces the roots of the 1903 split to debates over
  centralized party organization and anti-terrorist tactics predating What Is
  to Be Done?.
- Two-stage/Menshevik-stagist reading: 3 papers (Avila and Gaido; Day; Thatcher)
  -- treats the 1905 Revolution as a bourgeois-democratic stage requiring
  temporary cooperation with liberal opposition, and situates Menshevik stagism
  against Kautsky's and Trotsky's rival theses.
- Biographical/archival reading: 3 papers (Baron 1958; Baron 1981; Ascher review
  of Tiutiukin) -- reconstructs Plekhanov's positions on Russian development and
  wartime patriotism from primary and Soviet-era sources.
- General overview: 1 paper (Zhuravleva et al.) -- a brief conference-proceedings
  survey of Plekhanov's political career for a non-specialist audience.
====================================================================
}

@article{baron1958russiaimpact,
  author = {Baron, Samuel H.},
  title = {Plekhanov's Russia: The Impact of the West Upon an "Oriental" Society},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Ideas},
  year = {1958},
  volume = {19},
  number = {3},
  pages = {388},
  doi = {10.2307/2708043},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Baron reconstructs Plekhanov's account of Russia's historical
  development as a society shaped by Asiatic/"Oriental" state forms upon which
  Western capitalist relations were subsequently and unevenly imposed, a
  framework Plekhanov used to argue that Russia had entered, but not completed,
  the capitalist stage of development presupposed by orthodox Marxist theory.

  RELEVANCE: This is the theoretical ground of the "two-stage" schema examined
  in the project's key questions: Plekhanov's picture of Russia as a hybrid,
  belatedly capitalizing society supplied the premise that a bourgeois-
  democratic revolution had to precede any socialist one, and thus the
  historical-materialist warrant later invoked against Bolshevik seizure of
  power in 1917.

  POSITION: Historical reconstruction of Plekhanov's theory of Russian
  historical development as the foundation of Menshevik stagism.
  },
  keywords = {two-stage-theory, Russian-development, historical-materialism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{baron1981warrevolution,
  author = {Baron, Samuel H.},
  title = {Plekhanov in War and Revolution, 1914--17},
  journal = {International Review of Social History},
  year = {1981},
  volume = {26},
  number = {3},
  pages = {325--376},
  doi = {10.1017/S0020859000007215},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Baron traces Plekhanov's political conduct from the outbreak
  of World War I through the February and October Revolutions of 1917,
  documenting his adoption of "defencist" support for Russia's war effort, his
  consequent break with the Zimmerwald-aligned internationalist wing of Russian
  and European socialism, and his political isolation during 1917.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the project's question of how Plekhanov's WWI
  defencism is to be understood against his earlier internationalist Marxism;
  as a detailed archival study by Plekhanov's principal biographer, it supplies
  the empirical backbone for any account of the coherence (or lack of it)
  between his wartime patriotism and his prior theoretical commitments.

  POSITION: Biographical-historical reconstruction of Plekhanov's defencism and
  its reception, largely from a standpoint sympathetic to treating it as
  continuous with his prior political trajectory rather than as rupture.
  },
  keywords = {WWI-defencism, internationalism, Zimmerwald, biography, High},
  abstract = {Despite the fact that the views of Georgii Valentinovich Plekhanov (1856–1918) on the World War cannot be studied in his voluminous collected works – the editor abruptly terminated the edition with an article Plekhanov published on the eve of the war's outbreak – his position is broadly familiar to students of Russian and international socialism. Thanks to his substantial published writings from September 1914 to March 1917 scattered through the press of at least five countries, a two volume collection of his articles and speeches for the remainder of 1917, and several brief secondary accounts, it is possible to trace Plekhanov's wartime outlook. By contrast, little is known of his political initiatives, associations and conflicts during the war years. A considerable share of his correspondence and other personal papers – the kind of material likely to illuminate these aspects – has been printed by the Dom Plekhanova , the Leningrad repository that holds almost all of them.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{gaido2017iskragroup,
  author = {Gaido, Daniel and Jozami, Maximiliano},
  title = {El grupo Iskra y el "modelo leninista" de organización partidaria: Un debate en el seno de la socialdemocracia rusa},
  journal = {Izquierdas},
  year = {2017},
  number = {35},
  pages = {318--369},
  doi = {10.4067/s0718-50492017000400318},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Drawing on primary documents and Vera Zasulich's 1902 critique
  of Socialist-Revolutionary terrorism, the authors argue that the
  organizational model of Lenin's What Is to Be Done? (1902) was not simply an
  individual initiative but the collective project of the Iskra editorial
  board, adapting German Social Democratic practice to Russian conditions and
  to factional struggle against "Economists," the Bund, and local
  organizations, and that this same model of centralized party organization
  precipitated the 1903 Bolshevik/Menshevik split at the Second Congress of the
  RSDLP.

  RELEVANCE: Gives the organizational prehistory of Plekhanov's break with
  Lenin: Plekhanov sat on the Iskra editorial board and supported the
  centralizing project before siding with Martov's minority against Lenin's
  party-rules proposal at the Second Congress, so this paper supplies the
  concrete institutional sequence underlying the project's question of why the
  split occurred and how it maps onto rival theories of the party.

  POSITION: Historical-materialist reconstruction crediting the Iskra board
  collectively, not Lenin alone, with the "Leninist" model of party
  organization, and tracing its bifurcation into Bolshevik and Menshevik
  variants.
  },
  keywords = {Iskra, RSDLP-split, party-organization, Menshevism, High},
  abstract = {espanolEn marzo de 1902 Lenin publico un opusculo titulado ?Que hacer? Problemas candentes de nuestro movimiento, en el cual propuso un modelo de organizacion partidaria para el naciente movimiento socialdemocrata ruso, que debia organizarse a nivel nacional. Dicho libro ha planteado historicamente una serie de interrogantes, de los cuales los mas importantes son los siguientes: ?Estaba Lenin proponiendo un nuevo modelo de organizacion partidaria (un “partido de cuadros” en contraposicion a un “partido de masas”) o simplemente adaptando el ejemplo del Partido Socialdemocrata de Alemania (SPD, el “partido-guia” de la Segunda Internacional) a las condiciones de la clandestinidad rusa y de la lucha fraccional contra los “economistas”, el Bund judio y una serie de organizaciones locales? ?Representaba dicho modelo de organizacion partidaria una iniciativa individual de Lenin, o era el proyecto conjunto del grupo que editaba el periodico Iskra (La chispa), que venia publicandose desde el primero de diciembre de 1900 y que organizaria el Segundo Congreso del Partido Obrero Socialdemocrata de Rusia (POSDR) en julio-agosto de 1903? Finalmente: ?Que relacion existe entre el modelo de organizacion partidaria propuesto en el libro de Lenin y la subsecuente escision entre bolcheviques y mencheviques en el Segundo Congreso del POSDR? En el presente articulo intentaremos responder a estas preguntas a la luz de una serie de documentos primarios recientemente traducidos del ruso, asi como de la critica de Vera Zasulich, una de las representantes mas destacadas del grupo editorial de Iskra, a la tactica del terrorismo individual empleada por el recientemente creado Partido Socialista Revolucionario ruso, critica que fue publicada en el organo teorico de la Socialdemocracia alemana, Die neue Zeit, en diciembre de 1902. EnglishIn March 1902, Lenin published a book entitled What Is to Be Done? Burning Questions of Our Movement, proposing a model of party organization for the nascent Russian social democratic movement, which had to be organized nationally. The book has historically raised a number of questions, of which the most important are: Was Lenin proposing a new model of party organization (a "cadre party" as opposed to a "mass party") or simply adapting the example of the Social Democratic Party of Germany (SPD, the backbone of the Second International) to the conditions of Russian autocracy and to the factional struggle against the "economists", the Jewish Bund and a series of local organizations? Did that model of party organization represent an individual initiative of Lenin, or was it the joint project of the group that published the newspaper Iskra (Spark), which had been appearing since the first of December 1900 and which would organize the Second Congress of the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party (RSDLP) in July-August 1903? Finally, what was the connection between the model of party organization proposed in Lenin’s book and the subsequent split between Bolsheviks and Mensheviks at the Second Congress of the RSDLP? In this article we will try to answer these questions in the light of a number of primary documents recently translated from Russian as well as of the criticism by Vera Zasulich, one of the most prominent representatives of the Iskra editorial board, of the tactics of individual terrorism employed by the newly created Russian Socialist Revolutionary Party, a criticism published in the theoretical organ of the German Social Democracy, Die neue Zeit, in December 1902.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{avila2017kautsky,
  author = {Avila, Paula and Gaido, Daniel},
  title = {Karl Kautsky and the Russian Revolution of 1905: A Debate on the Driving Forces and the Prospects of the Russian Revolution in the Second International},
  journal = {International Critical Thought},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {7},
  number = {3},
  pages = {402--417},
  doi = {10.1080/21598282.2017.1323312},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: In October 1906 Plekhanov circulated a questionnaire to
  leaders of international Social Democracy asking about the class character
  of the 1905 Russian Revolution and the tactics it implied; twelve replies
  overwhelmingly endorsed the Menshevik view that the revolution was bourgeois
  (at most bourgeois-with-socialist-elements) and that cooperation with liberal
  opposition parties was therefore warranted, but Kautsky's reply rejected any
  coalition with the bourgeoisie and so undercut the Menshevik position from
  within the very poll Plekhanov had organized to confirm it.

  RELEVANCE: This is a direct primary-source reconstruction of Plekhanov
  soliciting theoretical validation for the two-stage schema and instead
  provoking a consequential internal challenge to it; it shows the "two-stage"
  theory being tested and contested in real time by the leading theorists of
  the Second International, bearing directly on the project's question of how
  the philosophy of history shaped Plekhanov's theory of Russian revolution.

  POSITION: Documents the Kautsky/Menshevik disagreement as an early fracture
  in the stagist consensus, later exploited by both Lenin and Trotsky for
  divergent theoretical purposes.
  },
  keywords = {two-stage-theory, Kautsky, Second-International, 1905-revolution, High},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT In October 1906 G. V. Plekhanov sent a questionnaire to the leaders of international Social Democracy, requesting their opinion about the class character of the Russian Revolution and the tactics which followed from this analysis for the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party. He received 12 responses, which overwhelmingly supported the Menshevik position: the Russian Revolution was not socialist, at most it was bourgeois revolution with socialist elements; the boycott of the Duma was considered an error and cooperation with the parties of the bourgeois opposition, essential. The exception to this rule was the reply of Karl Kautsky. While Kautsky’s answer was cautious enough for both Lenin and Trotsky to consider it as a confirmation of their theories, it categorically rejected the possibility of a coalition with the bourgeoisie, the posture favoured by Plekhanov, and therefore constituted a clear refutation of the Menshevik prospects on the Russian Revolution.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@incollection{day2009permanentrevolution,
  author = {Day, Richard B.},
  title = {Introduction -- The Historical Origin of the Expression `Permanent Revolution'},
  booktitle = {Witnesses to Permanent Revolution: The Documentary Record},
  year = {2009},
  pages = {1--58},
  publisher = {Brill},
  doi = {10.1163/EJ.9789004167704.I-684.5},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Day's introduction to this documentary collection traces the
  genealogy of "permanent revolution" as a technical term from Marx and Engels
  through the Second International debates to Trotsky and Parvus, situating it
  as a rival schema to the Menshevik/Plekhanovite two-stage theory of Russian
  revolution that separated a bourgeois-democratic phase from a subsequent
  socialist one.

  RELEVANCE: Supplies the comparative frame the project's key questions
  require for assessing Plekhanov's two-stage schema: by reconstructing the
  rival "permanent revolution" tradition documentarily, it lets Menshevik
  stagism be read as one contested position among several live options within
  Russian and international Marxism rather than as an uncontroversial
  deduction from historical materialism.

  POSITION: Editorial/historiographical introduction favoring the documentary
  reconstruction of the permanent-revolution tradition as a serious theoretical
  rival to stagism, rather than adjudicating between them.
  },
  keywords = {permanent-revolution, two-stage-theory, Trotsky, historiography, Medium},
  abstract = {This is the introduction chapter of the book, which reveals an array of opinions ranging from Plekhanov's conviction that a bourgeois revolution was pending-even if it must be led by the workers-to the opposite position shared by Ryazanov, Parvus and Trotsky, namely, that a permanent revolution would rapidly point beyond bourgeois democracy in the direction of socialism. Marxism emerged in Russia in a struggle against revolutionary Narodnik populism, but during and after the 1905 revolution the echoes of previous disputes with the Narodniks were still apparent in assessments of Russia's 'peculiar' characteristics given by Ryazanov, Trotsky, and even Lenin. Plekhanov and Lenin succeeded in denying the Bor'ba group any official representation at the Second Congress of the Russian Social-Democratic Labour Party, which was held in London and Brussels in the summer of 1903. This congress resulted in the famous split between Bolsheviks and Mensheviks over organisational issues. Keywords: bourgeois revolution; David Ryazanov; Georgy Plekhanov; Lenin; Leon Trotsky; Marxism; Narodnik populism; Parvus; permanent revolution; Russian Social-Democratic Labour Party},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{thatcher2021momentoftruth,
  author = {Thatcher, Ian D.},
  title = {The 1905--07 Russian Revolution as a `Moment of Truth': An Overlooked Contribution from Menshevism},
  journal = {Revolutionary Russia},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {34},
  number = {2},
  pages = {175--195},
  doi = {10.1080/09546545.2021.1984064},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Thatcher recovers a largely overlooked, projected five-part
  Menshevik collective study of the social forces active in the 1905
  Revolution (four of five volumes appeared in 1907, covering reaction, the
  proletariat, the peasantry, and the liberal/democratic bourgeoisie), arguing
  it represents the first sustained attempt from within Russian Marxism to
  analyze the revolution as a whole from a single (Menshevik) perspective, and
  that it anticipates interpretations later developed in the more famous
  1909--14 Menshevik history of the same period.

  RELEVANCE: Provides evidence for how Menshevik theorists (not Plekhanov
  alone) operationalized the two-stage schema empirically, class by class, in
  analyzing 1905 -- material the project can use to assess how far "Menshevism"
  followed distinctively from Plekhanov's own theory as opposed to a broader
  collective Menshevik research program.

  POSITION: Recovers an internal Menshevik body of empirical class analysis as
  a serious and continuous contribution to Russian Marxist historiography of
  1905, rather than treating Menshevik theory as static doctrine.
  },
  keywords = {Menshevism, 1905-revolution, class-analysis, Medium},
  abstract = {This article is the first exposition of a projected five-part Menshevik study of social forces in the Russian Revolution of 1905, only four volumes of which appeared in 1907 covering reaction, the proletariat, the peasantry, and the liberal and democratic bourgeoisie. This collective effort marked perhaps the first attempt to present an overall analysis of the revolution from within one perspective, that of the Menshevik variety of Russian Marxism. Despite the centrality of perceptions of revolution to participants and future historians of Russian socialism and of 1905, this project has been largely overlooked. This is to be regretted, for the volumes contain interpretations now familiar on the nature of the 1905 revolution and why it failed. Furthermore, there is continuity between the works and authors of 1907 and the subsequent (1909–14) much more famous Menshevik history of social movements in Russia at the beginning of the twentieth century.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{gaido2015zasulich,
  author = {Gaido, Daniel and Bosch Alessio, Constanza Daniela},
  title = {Vera Zasulich's Critique of Neo-Populism: Party Organisation and Individual Terrorism in the Russian Revolutionary Movement (1878--1902)},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {23},
  number = {4},
  pages = {93--125},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206X-12341441},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The authors reconstruct Vera Zasulich's trajectory from her
  1878 assassination attempt on Governor Trepov, through her role as one of the
  five founders of the Emancipation of Labour group in 1883, to her 1902
  polemic against the newly formed Socialist-Revolutionary Party's revival of
  individual-terrorist tactics -- a polemic that supported the organizational
  plans for the RSDLP that Lenin was developing in What Is to Be Done?.

  RELEVANCE: Establishes the Emancipation of Labour group's founding polemic
  (against Narodnik individual terrorism, in favor of organized mass party
  politics) as the shared starting point from which Plekhanov's later
  disagreement with Lenin over the *form* of that same organizational project
  emerged; useful background for the project's question of how the 1903 split
  relates to Plekhanov's own founding commitments.

  POSITION: Situates Zasulich (and by extension the Emancipation of Labour
  group) as originally aligned with, not opposed to, the centralizing
  organizational logic Lenin later formalized -- complicating any simple
  narrative in which Menshevism was anti-organizational from the outset.
  },
  keywords = {Emancipation-of-Labour, party-organization, Narodism, Medium},
  abstract = {Vera Zasulich’s shooting of Trepov, a governor of St Petersburg who had ordered the flogging of a political prisoner, in January 1878, catapulted her to international fame as a revolutionary heroine, a reputation that she put to good use by becoming one of the five ‘founding parents’ of Russian Marxism that created the ‘Group for the Emancipation of Labour’ in 1883. But her act of self-sacrifice also triggered, to her dismay, the institutionalisation of individual-terrorist tactics in the Russian Populist movement with the creation of the ‘People’s Will’ (Narodnaya Volya) Party in 1879. The organisation went into decline after the killing of Tsar Alexander II in 1881, and Populism itself was increasingly superseded by Marxism as the hegemonic force on the left with the rise of the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party (RSDLP). But individual-terrorist tactics reappeared with the creation of the Socialist Revolutionary Party in 1902, prompting Zasulich to write an article for Die neue Zeit, the theoretical organ of German Social Democracy, in which she both condemned the Neo-Populist tendency as deleterious to the rising labour movement and supported the organisational plans for the RSDLP sponsored by the Iskra group, developed at length by Lenin in his book What Is to Be Done?, published in March 1902. This article provides the background to Vera Zasulich’s article ‘The Terrorist Tendency in Russia’ (December 1902), setting it against the history of the Russian revolutionary movement from 1878 to 1902.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{ascher1984tiutiukinreview,
  author = {Ascher, Abraham},
  title = {Pervaia rossiiskaia revoliutsiia i G. V. Plekhanov: Iz istorii ideinoi bor'by v rabochem dvizhenii Rossii v 1905--1907 gg.},
  journal = {The American Historical Review},
  year = {1984},
  volume = {89},
  number = {1},
  pages = {172},
  doi = {10.2307/1856027},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This review by the leading Western historian of Menshevism
